Subject: [PATCH] feat: privacy, consent & device permission controls (Task
 0013)

Neither Task 0010 nor Task 0012 is merged into this base; this change
is fully self-contained and depends on neither.

## Core gap found and fixed

The existing nearby-medicine-search feature already had genuinely
privacy-safe geolocation handling: location was only requested on an
explicit "Find near me" click (never automatic), and coordinates were
already used transiently and never persisted. But it went straight
from that click to the browser's native permission prompt, with no
app-controlled explanation first -- this task's central, explicit
requirement. Fixed by inserting a mandatory explanation step, without
rebuilding the underlying search feature.

## New reusable primitives

- lib/geolocation-permission.ts: SSR-safe hook. Distinguishes
  "unsupported" (no Geolocation API) from "unknown" (Permissions API
  itself unsupported, e.g. Safari) from "prompt"/"granted"/"denied".
  Never calls the browser API on mount. requestLocation() resolves a
  full, distinct outcome taxonomy (granted/denied/unavailable/timeout/
  unsupported) rather than conflating denial with unavailability.
- lib/notification-permission.ts: same SSR-safe pattern for
  Notification.permission/requestPermission.
- components/permission-explanation-dialog.tsx: the reusable app-
  controlled explanation shown before any browser permission prompt.
  Never hides the decline option, never makes Continue visually
  deceptive, never auto-dismisses.

## Nearby search integration (not rebuilt)

public-medicine-search.tsx: "Find near me" now opens the explanation
dialog; only its own "Use my location" click proceeds to
requestLocation(). Updated the three existing tests that assumed the
old immediate-prompt behavior (a legitimate behavioral fix, not a
weakening -- verified each still asserts the same underlying
guarantees) and added two new tests proving the browser API is never
called before the explanation, and never called at all if declined.

## Consent model (new)

ConsentRecord (Prisma model + migration): append-only, enforced by a
database trigger mirroring the existing StockMovement immutability
convention exactly -- a withdrawal is always a NEW row with status
WITHDRAWN, never an update/delete of the historical GRANTED row. The
user's current effective consent for a category is always the single
latest row for that (userId, category) pair; there is deliberately no
separate "current state" table to drift out of sync with this log.
Categories: LOCATION_USE, NOTIFICATIONS_RESERVATIONS,
NOTIFICATIONS_OPERATIONAL.

Migration cross-checked programmatically in both directions: the
ConsentRecord schema fields exactly match the migration's CREATE TABLE
columns, and the full audit-event type list in the migration's updated
CHECK constraint exactly matches packages/database/src/audit.ts's
TypeScript allowlist.

New consent module (apps/auth-service/src/consent/): repository
(append-only writes; latest-per-category reads), service (records
GRANTED/WITHDRAWN events, audits each via the existing AuditWriter --
metadata contains only the bounded category name, never the source
string, never coordinates, never notification content), controller
(GET/POST /users/me/consent, self-scoped via @CurrentIdentity() with
no PermissionsGuard, matching the existing me/privacy convention
exactly -- this is the caller's own preference data, not an action on
another identity). Wired into AppModule.

## App preference vs browser permission (kept distinct throughout)

UserPrivacy gains two new MedSphere-preference fields
(wantsReservationNotifications, wantsOperationalAlerts) -- these are
never conflated with browser Notification.permission state anywhere:
the new DevicePermissionsSection always reads and displays browser
capability state separately from these preference toggles, and a
granted app preference never implies the browser permission was
granted (verified by test).

## Settings UI

- Two new preference toggles added to the existing, already-accessible
  privacy toggle list (reused, not rebuilt).
- New DevicePermissionsSection: shows browser-reported location/
  notification permission state as read-only badges (StatusBadge,
